dwm - dynamic window manager
============================
dwm is an extremely fast, small, and dynamic window manager for X.


Requirements
------------
In order to build dwm you need Xlib, Xft, Fontconfig, Xinerama,
XComposite, XDamage and XRender development headers and libraries.


Installation
------------
Edit config.mk to match your local setup (dwm is installed into
the /usr/local namespace by default).

Afterwards enter the following command to build and install dwm (if
necessary as root):

    make clean install


Running dwm
-----------
Add the following line to your .xinitrc to start dwm using startx:

    exec dwm

In order to connect dwm to a specific display, make sure that
the DISPLAY environment variable is set correctly, e.g.:

    DISPLAY=foo.bar:1 exec dwm

(This will start dwm on display :1 of the host foo.bar.)

The optional standalone CUI bar supplies its own status information; see
"Standalone CUI bar" below. It does not read xsetroot status text.

Configuration
-------------
The configuration of dwm is done by creating a custom config.h
and (re)compiling the source code.

Fixed-size application windows
------------------------------
Applications that advertise equal minimum and maximum dimensions through X11
size hints open floating at those dimensions instead of being stretched by the
tiling layout. This also applies if the application publishes its fixed-size
hints after opening. Explicit fullscreen requests still work; exiting fullscreen
restores the fixed size. Applications that do not advertise fixed-size X11
hints remain governed by the normal tiling rules. Rebuild and restart dwm to
apply this behavior.

Window switcher
---------------
Alt+Tab opens a fast, most-recently-used list of windows on the current
monitor, including windows on other tags. Keep Alt held and press Tab (or
Shift+Tab) to cycle; release Alt or press Enter to select. Escape cancels.
Press Delete to close the highlighted window without switching to its tag; the
switcher stays open. Apps can prompt to save changes after you release Alt.
As with Alt+C, apps without WM_DELETE_WINDOW support are forcibly closed.
While the switcher is open, press 1–9 to preview/filter that tag's windows,
or 0 to show all tags. Selecting a hidden window switches to its tag. The
popup shows a live preview of the selected window using XComposite, XDamage
and XRender, alongside window titles. It uses the Quickshell palette and
monospace font. If the X server lacks those extensions, titles still work. Alt+Ctrl+Tab retains the previous-tag shortcut.
Rebuild and restart dwm after changing the configuration.

Monitor switcher
----------------
With at least two monitors, hold Alt and press ` (grave) to open the monitor
switcher. It previews the windows on each monitor's currently visible tags in
a miniature layout and lists their titles. Press `, Tab or the arrow keys to
choose a display; Shift reverses cycling. Release Alt or press Enter to focus
the selected monitor and move the pointer there; Escape cancels. For more
than three monitors, the cards page automatically as you cycle. The previews
use the same X extensions and fallback as the window switcher. Existing
Alt+comma and Alt+period monitor focus shortcuts still work.

Wallpaper switcher
------------------
Alt+Shift+W opens a built-in X11 wallpaper switcher, drawn by dwm in the same
style as Alt+Tab. There is no separate GTK application to install or launch.
It scans ~/Downloads/wallpaper (the folder used by your Hyprland config) when
opened. Use Up/Down or j/k (or the mouse) to choose an image, Enter or the
SET WALLPAPER button to apply it, R to rescan, and Escape to cancel. Supported
extensions are jpg, jpeg, png, webp, bmp and gif. The large preview shows the
selected image when Imlib2 and its appropriate image loader are installed;
if Imlib2 is unavailable, the list and selection still work.

Install feh and the Imlib2 runtime, then rebuild and restart dwm with
`make clean install`. The switcher executes `feh --bg-fill` for the selected
file on the X11 screens. feh saves ~/.fehbg; to restore the choice at login,
add `[ -x "$HOME/.fehbg" ] && "$HOME/.fehbg" &` to your dwm autostart script
or .xinitrc. To use another folder, set DWM_WALLPAPERS_DIR to an absolute
path in the environment before starting dwm. Missing or unreadable folders
are reported in the popup; create the folder and press R to retry. The
switcher does not change Hyprland's wallpaper configuration.

Display modes, keyboard layout and wallpaper restore
----------------------------------------------------
Alt+Shift+R opens a native dwm popup: select a connected monitor, then a
resolution and refresh rate. Arrow keys and Enter or mouse clicks select;
Escape cancels. dwm calls xrandr and saves a successful choice in
${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.config}/refreshrate.conf, compatible with your
existing file. No refreshmenu script or rofi is needed. To restore modes at
login, replace the old refreshmenu --apply line in your autostart with:

    dwm --apply-display-settings

This is a one-shot command, not a second window manager. Run it after your
xrandr positioning command and before restoring the wallpaper. xrandr must be
installed and dwm must be on PATH; failures are printed to the session log.

Win+Space switches the XKB keyboard group. If your existing XKB options
include `grp:win_space_toggle`, dwm leaves that shortcut to XKB; otherwise
dwm handles it directly. It watches for keyboard configuration changes and
does not edit existing autostart files or clear your other XKB options.
Both layouts must already be configured; the generated autostart sets
`us,ir` on a fresh setup. Existing autostart files are left untouched. If
you use different layouts, keep configuring them as you already do.

Your old hard-coded `feh --bg-fill ...` autostart line resets the wallpaper
on every login. Replace it rather than adding another feh command beside it:

    if [ -x "$HOME/.fehbg" ]; then
        "$HOME/.fehbg" &
    else
        feh --bg-fill "$HOME/Downloads/wallpaper/glassmorphsesimjapon.png" &
    fi

Run this *after* the xrandr positioning and dwm --apply-display-settings commands.
The picker uses feh, which writes ~/.fehbg on a successful change. See
`autostart.example.sh` for an example based on your current setup; merge it
into your existing dwm autostart instead of running both copies. The example
assumes the same eDP-1 and HDMI-1 output names as your current setup.

The wallpaper preview now decodes only the highlighted image in a separate,
cancellable helper and transfers a scaled preview back to dwm. Moving through
high-resolution files should not block the dwm event loop; applying via feh
can still take a few seconds for large images.

Automatic first-run setup
-------------------------
On startup, dwm creates missing files itself; no setup script is needed. It
chooses $XDG_DATA_HOME/dwm (or ~/.local/share/dwm), falling back to ~/.dwm
if the XDG directory does not exist and ~/.dwm already exists. If neither
exists it creates the XDG directory. Within that directory it creates an
executable autostart.sh with the monitor, saved-mode, wallpaper, network
applet and keyboard setup shown in autostart.example.sh, and an empty
executable autostart_blocking.sh for optional blocking commands. It also
creates an empty ${XDG_CONFIG_HOME:-$HOME/.config}/refreshrate.conf and the
default ~/Downloads/wallpaper directory if they do not exist. If
DWM_WALLPAPERS_DIR is set, the custom directory is not created automatically.

Existing files, symlinks, directories and permissions are never overwritten
or changed by first-run setup, including non-executable autostart files.
Existing executable autostart scripts continue to run as before. If an
existing autostart still contains the old hard-coded feh command, it can
reset the wallpaper at login; update that line manually if needed. Existing
Win+Space XKB settings are handled automatically as described above.
See the preceding section for the replacements. feh writes ~/.fehbg only
after a wallpaper is chosen; dwm does not create or overwrite it at startup.
Dwm does not install dependencies or rebuild itself. Setup failures are
reported in the session log; startup continues where possible.

Control center comparison
-------------------------
Two versions are available for testing. Both are compiled into dwm, not
launched from a separate dashboard executable:

* Alt+Shift+D: native Xlib/Drw popup, like the other dwm switchers. Use
  Left/Right or Tab to change section, Up/Down to select, Enter to act, and
  Escape to close. Clicking rows and scrolling are also supported. Select a
  Bluetooth device first, then select an action further down the list.
* Ctrl+Alt+Shift+D: CUI version, if built with CUI=1 as described below. Its
  interface is compiled into dwm; its event loop runs in a forked child with
  a separate floating X11 window so the window manager remains responsive.
  It uses the same sidebar and redesigned overview as the bar dashboard;
  narrow windows use compact icon navigation.

Both use nmcli (NetworkManager), bluetoothctl (BlueZ), and pactl (PulseAudio
or PipeWire's PulseAudio compatibility service). These services must be
running and the desktop user must have permission to control them. Operations
run outside dwm's event loop and refresh periodically. Wi-Fi passwords are
sent through private pipes, not command-line arguments, and cleared after
submission. The password field supports WPA personal; use NetworkManager's
connection editor for enterprise credentials or hidden networks. Bluetooth
pairing requiring PIN or confirmation needs a separate running pairing agent,
for example Blueman. Backend and permission failures are displayed.

The native version is part of the normal dwm build and needs no extra UI
library. To additionally build the CUI version, install CMake, Git, a C
compiler, pthreads, and the X11 development libraries required by CUI. From
config/dwm, fetch and build the pinned CUI revision, then build dwm:

    cmake -S dashboard -B dashboard/build
    cmake --build dashboard/build --target cui
    make clean
    make CUI=1
    sudo make CUI=1 install

Restart dwm after installation. The CUI version prefers installed Vazirmatn, or
uses the system monospace font via fc-match and CUI's built-in mono;
DWM_DASHBOARD_FONT may name
a font file. Its optional settings buttons launch nm-connection-editor,
blueman-manager, and pavucontrol when installed. The standalone CUI build
remains available for independent testing, but is not needed by the binding.
Existing autostart files are not changed.

Persian text
------------
The CUI bar, its dashboard and the optional CUI dashboard build with Arabic
text shaping enabled. Install a Persian-capable TrueType/OpenType font (such as
DejaVu Sans or Noto Sans Arabic) and fontconfig's fc-match. The UIs select a
Persian fallback with fc-match; CUI_PERSIAN_FONT can instead name the fallback
font file. When Vazirmatn is installed and visible to fontconfig, it is preferred
for UI text (including Latin); run fc-cache -f if it was just installed. Explicit
CUI_BAR_FONT and DWM_DASHBOARD_FONT settings retain priority in their respective
contexts. Native dwm panels (including Alt+Tab, wallpaper and the native dashboard) use
FriBidi for Persian joining and bidirectional ordering when libfribidi.so.0 is
installed. Native panels also prefer Vazirmatn over their default monospace font
when it is installed, without replacing a custom configured first font. Install
the FriBidi runtime package and rebuild/restart dwm.
Without it, the native panels retain their previous unshaped rendering.
This adds text rendering, not a Persian translation of the interface. CUI's
editable fields currently do not implement RTL caret navigation or shaping;
native password input is also limited by XLookupString. Other applications and
Dunst manage their own font and shaping settings independently.

Standalone CUI bar
------------------
The old dwm bar and tray have been removed. The replacement is a separate,
lightweight CUI/X11 application under ../cui-bar. It has its own XEmbed tray,
and communicates with dwm only for per-monitor workspace state, workspace
clicks, and reserving screen space. It does not depend on the dashboard
build. When `cui-bar` is on dwm's PATH, dwm starts it automatically if no
other X11 tray is running; existing autostart files are left unchanged. Set
DWM_NO_CUI_BAR=1 before starting dwm to disable automatic launch.

See ../cui-bar/README for build instructions, controls, and limitations.

The CUI dashboard and the bar's control popup also include a display mode
page. Display changes use xrandr and the same saved per-output configuration
as the native switcher. Wallpaper selection remains in the native picker and
uses feh and its ~/.fehbg.
The native wallpaper picker previews images with Imlib2 (including its image
loaders), and remains open after applying so another can be selected. If the
default folder is empty it also checks ~/Pictures/Wallpapers,
~/Pictures/wallpapers and ~/wallpapers. DWM_WALLPAPERS_DIR explicitly selects
a folder and disables this fallback.
